---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Artifacts and Staged Database SQL — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-sky">For DevOps</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Artifacts and Staged Database SQL</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <p>
                Cross-connection and large intermediates cannot travel through messages or
                in-memory step payloads. Artifacts are first-class outputs with schema
                fingerprint, checksum, retention, producer, and sensitivity. Parquet is the
                tabular baseline. JSON/text/SQL/diagnostics may also be stored.
            </p>

            <h2>Store</h2>
            <p>
                Local filesystem for development. S3-compatible object storage for the
                production baseline. Both implement the same store contracts. New object
                keys prefix the project id; existing files keep their stored URI.
            </p>

            <h2>Produce → transform → consume</h2>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Produce</strong> — DatabaseQueryToParquet runs SQL on a source connection and writes a file, <em>or</em> an <code class="text-foreground">external-parquet</code> root is seeded from an upload at trigger time. Metadata records run, step, and location. <a href="/docs/developer-external-parquet" class="text-link hover:underline">External Parquet ingest</a>.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Transform</strong> — an eligible agent runs DuckDB SQL against one or more Parquet inputs and writes a new artifact.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Consume</strong> — Staged Database SQL creates staging tables, bulk-loads with provider-native APIs, then runs developer-authored SQL that joins stages using logical symbols. There is no ArtifactToTable mapping; configuration that implies one-to-one automatic table copy is rejected.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Cross-connection</h2>
            <p>
                Fan-out from one source to SQL Server and PostgreSQL is a supported shape:
                each destination is a separate connection and sink step. There are no
                distributed transactions across those connections. Artifact-aware scheduling
                avoids starting a sink when the Parquet file is not ready.
            </p>
            <p>
                Fan-in of Parquet into Artifact SQL or Staged Database SQL is always
                <strong class="text-foreground">Wait all</strong>. Wait any is rejected
                on those handlers, on <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code>
                bindings, and on <code class="text-foreground">artifact_available</code>
                edges. There is no first-winner artifact. Control-only OR joins belong
                on classic SQL after alternative extracts —
                <a href="/docs/developer-join-mode" class="text-link hover:underline">Wait all and Wait any</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
